The '''2016 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League]], the 61st season of Europe's premier club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 24th season since it was renamed from the European Champion Clubs' Cup to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[San Siro|Stadio San Siro]] in [[Milan]], [[Italy]], on 28 May 2016,<ref name="2016 host">{{cite news |title=Milan to host 2016 UEFA Champions League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c510486f1c5-5e2186361d74-1000--milan-to-host-2016-uefa-champions-league-final/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 September 2014 |access-date=19 September 2014 }}</ref> between Spanish teams [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] and [[Atlético Madrid]], in a repeat of the [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014 final]]. It was the second time in the tournament's history that both finalists were from the same city. Real Madrid won 5–3 on a penalty shoot-out after a 1–1 draw at the end of extra time, securing a record-extending 11th title in the competition.

Real Madrid earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2015–16 UEFA Europa League]], [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], in the [[2016 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified to enter the semi-finals of the [[2016 FIFA Club World Cup]] as the UEFA representative, ultimately triumphing in both competitions.

==Venue==
[[File:Stadio Giuseppe Meazza - San Siro.jpg|thumb|The Stadio San Siro in Milan was selected to host the final in September 2014.]]
The [[San Siro|Stadio San Siro]], officially known as Stadio Giuseppe Meazza, was announced as the venue of the final at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Nyon]], Switzerland, on 18 September 2014,<ref name="2016 host"/> the fourth European Cup/Champions League final hosted at the stadium following those in [[1965 European Cup final|1965]], [[1970 European Cup final|1970]] and [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]].{{citation needed|date=June 2020}}

The San Siro was built in 1925, opened in 1926 as the home of [[AC Milan|Milan]], and was sold to the city in 1935. [[Inter Milan|Internazionale]] became tenants in 1947, and the stadium has been shared by the two clubs ever since, with Inter winning the first European Cup final played at the stadium in 1965. The stadium was used as a venue in the [[1934 FIFA World Cup]], [[UEFA Euro 1980]], and the [[1990 FIFA World Cup]]. Its current capacity is 80,018, but is reduced to just under 80,000 seats for UEFA competitions.<ref name="2016 host"/>

The 2016 final marked the first time a final has been held at the San Siro when neither of its tenants were able to win the competition, as Milan and Internazionale both failed to qualify for any European competitions after their performance in the [[2014–15 Serie A]].{{citation needed|date=June 2020}}

==Background==
[[File:Zidane_Zizu.jpg|thumb|upright|Real Madrid manager [[Zinedine Zidane]] won the Champions League while playing for the club in 2002]]

This final was the sixth tournament final to feature two teams from the same association,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d800ac7b06d-5206de193190-1000--one-nation-champions-league-finals/|title=One-nation UEFA Champions League finals|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=21 May 2016}}</ref> the third all-Spanish final, and the second between teams from the same city, fielding exactly the two teams that faced each other in the [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014 final]], making it the seventh repeated final pairing.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c715cad7-29a5fa4a1a1b-1000--second-time-round-european-cup-final-rematches/|title=Second time round: European Cup final rematches|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=9 May 2016}}</ref> The [[Madrid Derby|all-Madrid final]] also guaranteed the city of [[Madrid]] becoming the most successful city in the European Cup with 11 wins and 17 final appearances, and also in all UEFA club competitions with 16 wins, overtaking the city of [[Milan]] with 10 wins and 16 final appearances in the European Cup and 15 wins in all UEFA club competitions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/022d-0e957c15b010-cfdab9a6dbdb-1000--madrid-supplants-milan-as-top-footballing-city/|title=Madrid to supplant Milan as top footballing city|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=6 May 2016}}</ref>

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] reached a record 14th final after a 1–0 aggregate win against [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]], with a chance to win a [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics|record 11th title]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36126021|title=Real Madrid 1–0 Manchester City (agg 1–0)|date=4 May 2016|work=BBC Sport}}</ref> Previously, they won finals in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], and 2014, and lost in [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]]. This was also their 18th final in all UEFA club competitions, having also played in two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1983]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup Final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup Final|1986]]). Their manager, [[Zinedine Zidane]], who scored the winning goal for Real Madrid in the 2002 final, was aiming to become the seventh man to win the Champions League as both player and manager,<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/uefa-champions-league/zinedine-zidane-aiming-to-become-seventh-to-win-uefa-champions-league-as-player-and-manager/news-story/373c01dcf47bd93ea3c8cdab2f7f1a95 | title=Zinedine Zidane aiming to become seventh to win UEFA Champions League as player and manager | work=[[Fox Sports]] | date=25 May 2016 | access-date=28 May 2016}}</ref> joining [[Miguel Muñoz]], [[Giovanni Trapattoni]], [[Johan Cruyff]], [[Carlo Ancelotti]], [[Frank Rijkaard]] and [[Pep Guardiola]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/022d-0e90fc10974b-cb5bd86b8f7d-1000--who-has-won-the-uefa-champions-league-as-a-player-and-coach/|title=Will Zidane be latest winner as a player and coach?|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=22 May 2016}}</ref> The team had had a disastrous first half of the season, being left out of contention to win La Liga, sacking newly-appointed [[Rafael Benítez]] in January.

[[Atlético Madrid]] reached their third European Cup final after defeating [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] on away goals (2–2 on aggregate).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36126014|title=Bayern Munich 2–1 Atletico Madrid (agg 2–2)|date=3 May 2016|work=BBC Sport}}</ref> Their previous two European Cup finals in [[1974 European Cup final|1974]] and 2014 both ended in defeats, to Bayern Munich and Real Madrid respectively. Atlético Madrid had also played in three [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (winning in [[1962 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1962]], and losing in [[1963 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1963]] and [[1986 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1986]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] finals (winning in [[2010 UEFA Europa League Final|2010]] and [[2012 UEFA Europa League Final|2012]]), with their most recent Europa League triumph in 2012 led by current coach [[Diego Simeone]], widely attributed as having brought Atlético Madrid back to glory, but had yet to win a Champions League. He had the chance to join fellow Argentinians [[Luis Carniglia]] and [[Helenio Herrera]] as the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers#By nationality|only non-European coaches]] to win the European Cup/Champions League.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/022d-0e9c6da5ed0a-01cc79f033c4-1000--atletico-coach-simeone-eyes-exclusive-club/|title=Atlético coach Simeone eyes exclusive club|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=23 May 2016}}</ref> Had they won the final, they would have become the first Spanish club and the fifth club overall to [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the main European club competitions|have won all three major European trophies]] at the time ([[UEFA Champions League|European Cup/Champions League]], UEFA Cup/Europa League and the now-defunct Cup Winners' Cup).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2016/E/E_2015789_PK.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171008080635/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2016/E/E_2015789_PK.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 8, 2017|title=2016 UEFA Champions League Final Press Kit|date=27 May 2016|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=27 May 2016}}</ref> On the other hand, if they were to lose, they would become the first team to lose their first three European Cup finals.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/022d-0e9475a3f13d-96d6cbb93192-1000--can-atletico-make-it-third-time-lucky-in-milan/|title=Can Atlético make it third time lucky in Milan?|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=27 May 2016}}</ref>

Apart from the 2014 final, won by Real Madrid 4–1 after extra time, the only previous Madrid Derby matches in European competitions were in the [[1958–59 European Cup]] semi-finals, where Real Madrid won 2–1 in a replay, after a 2–2 aggregate draw, and in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]] quarter-finals, where Real Madrid won 1–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e943e809e43-01d6f1c4a2b3-1000--past-meetings-real-madrid-v-atletico-in-europe/|title=Real Madrid v Atlético in European competition|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=4 May 2016}}</ref>

